## 2. Brief-derived review rubric (acceptance checklist for any research proposal under this brief)

Each item cites `inputs/brief.md` (B-Ln) or `inputs/output_contract.md` (C-Ln). A next candidate can score a proposal item-by-item; "missing" here means the proposal failed to address it, not that the idea is wrong.

- **R1 Architecture** — one coherent component choice or explicitly bounded alternatives (B-L19).
- **R2 Minimum workflow** — all nine steps covered: create project; import/identify data without unnecessary copying; author notebook/transform; bounded preview; interactive run; clean replay; provenance comparison; save/reopen on another machine; export results + small reproducibility manifest (B-L9).
- **R3 Record model** — the proposal must define: project record, environment binding, source-data identity, execution events, output association (B-L9).
- **R4 Data contract** — schema assumptions, type conversions, row/order identity, error handling; no silent deletion of malformed records; no mutation of originals; preview sampling explicitly not proof of a full-dataset property (B-L5).
- **R5 Notebook semantics** — displayed order vs execution order vs live kernel state vs saved-output provenance distinguished; "a saved output is not automatically reproducible"; clean-replay guarantee enumerated against nondeterminism, external services, unpinned packages, undeclared file access (B-L7).
- **R6 Status & invalidation** — per-output states current/stale/failed/unverified; cancel + progress + errors; defined invalidation when an upstream cell, source dataset, or environment dependency changes; kernel restart or interrupted run must not silently leave outputs marked current (B-L7).
- **R7 Envelope** — 5 GB tabular input through a bounded/streaming path where the operation allows; explicit different bound for unsupported global ops; framed as target-to-investigate, not verified result (B-L11).
- **R8 Operational semantics** — schema evolution; caching + invalidation; large-result truncation; deterministic vs stable ordering; concurrency/file locking; recovery after interrupted save (B-L11).
- **R9 Exchange & scope** — exchange via ordinary folders or portable bundles; credentials/private datasets/shared profiles declared out of scope (B-L11).
- **R10 Untrusted execution boundary** — filesystem/network/resource permissions with usability trade-offs; checks only via the admitted isolated execution; source browsing kept separate from execution; no claim that an import blacklist supplies isolation (B-L13).
- **R11 Support boundary** — a chosen Python support boundary and a defensible optional-SQL relationship; small team not required to build every kernel (B-L9).
- **R12 Precedents** — ≥2 independently useful implementation precedents with the mechanism each contributes and an independence argument (B-L17).
- **R13 Failure chain** — ≥1 consequential real issue followed through fix and regression test, with version/branch applicability and evidence limits; an issue title, API example, or merged patch alone must not be presented as release behavior (B-L17).
- **R14 Interchange format** — standard document format investigated with the explicit caveat that adoption does not settle runtime behavior (B-L9).
- **R15 Labeling discipline** — source facts / engineering inference / product choices / executed checks / proposed-UNEXECUTED kept separate; no invented receipts (C-L7).
- **R16 Ideas kept visible** — ≥1 useful opportunity and ≥1 plausible alternative; rejected alternatives recorded without becoming requirements (B-L15, B-L19).
- **R17 Catalogs** — sources.json records URL, pin, exact locator, capture identity, applicability; catalogs support but do not replace in-proposal citations (C-L5).
- **R18 Witness scope honesty** — synthetic/tiny-data witnesses labeled as component checks that do not prove large-data or full-runtime correctness (B-L13).

