### F-B — pinned notebook format anchor: nbformat v4.5 JSON schema
Capture: `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jupyter/nbformat/main/nbformat/v4/nbformat.v4.schema.json`, HTTP 200, 16104 B, sha256 `523e3578ddbfcad52933d2423dc5951114ef73f48df180b6a601498ba5ca071a`, ETag `5a268c84…`, branch `main` (moving pin) captured 2026-10-06T04:54:01Z. Verified locators: description "Jupyter Notebook v4.5 JSON schema"; `code_cell` requires `["id","cell_type","metadata","source","outputs","execution_count"]`; `execution_count` — "The code cell's prompt number. **Will be null if the cell has not been run**", type `["integer","null"]`; `outputs` is an array oneOf `execute_result | display_data | stream | error` (error requires `ename`, `evalue`, `traceback`); root `metadata.kernelspec` requires only `name`+`display_name`, `language_info` requires only `name`; cell `id` pattern `^[a-zA-Z0-9-_]+$`, 1–64 chars; cell `metadata.execution` stores iopub/shell message dates.
**Consequences for the next candidate:** (i) outputs and per-cell execution evidence persist inside the `.ipynb` document itself, and "never executed" is representable (`execution_count: null`) — this supports distinguishing displayed order, execution order, and saved-output provenance (R5); (ii) the schema captures **no package versions, no dataset revisions, no kernel live state** — kernelspec has no version requirement — so adopting `.ipynb` satisfies R14's caveat: format adoption does not settle runtime behavior; staleness/invalidation and manifest layers must be product-level metadata keyed by the stable cell `id` (R6); (iii) `error` outputs are first-class, so "failed" is representable without external bookkeeping.
**Limits:** a JSON schema constrains documents, not library behavior; `main` is a moving branch — the pin is the capture sha256 + date, and a tag-pinned capture (e.g. an nbformat release tag) is **proposed/UNEXECUTED**.

